roposition 5.14.6 . In particular, $\ell ^*_{-\infty }=\phi $ has positive mass. $\square$ Theorem 17.13.21 . B28F0A28CB3E The Legendre transform in definition 17.13.1 is a bijection 6D5BA4827FF5 17.13.22  egin{equation}  \mathrm{TC}(X,	heta ;\phi )\xrightarrow {\sim } \mathcal{R}(X,	heta ;\phi ). \end{equation} Moreover, this bijection restricts to the following bijections: AE510D6D0C2F 17.13.23  egin{equation}  \mathrm{TC}^1(X,	heta ;\phi )\xrightarrow {\sim } \mathcal{R}^1(X,	heta ;\phi ),\quad \mathrm{TC}^{\infty }(X,	heta ;\phi )\xrightarrow {\sim } \mathcal{R}^{\infty }(X,	heta ;\phi ). \end{equation} For any $\Gamma \in \mathrm{TC}^1(X,	heta ;\phi )$, we have 4884EC5C8FA9 17.13.24  egin{equation}  \mathbf{E}^{\phi }(\Gamma )=\mathbf{E}^{\phi }(\Gamma ^*). \end{equation} Recall that the two energy functionals in ( 17.13.24 ) are defined in ( 17.12.12 ) and definition 17.10.82 respectively. The correspondence ( 17.13.22 ) will be referred to as the Ross–Witt Nyström correspondence . To appreciate this result, just consider the simple case where $	heta $ is a Kähler form and $\phi =0$. In this case, elements in $\mathcal{R}^{\infty }(X,	heta )$ are rays of bounded potentials, while elements in $\mathrm{TC}^{\infty }(X,	heta )$ are rays of singular potentials. This result establishes a bridge between the pluripotential theory of regular potentials and that of singular potentials! Proof. Step 1 . We first establish ( 17.13.22 ). It follows from 17.13.11 that the forward map is well-defined. The inverse map is given by ( 17.13.14 ). We show that the inverse map is also well-defined. Given $\ell \in \mathcal{R}(X,	heta ;\phi )$, we know from 17.13.15 that $\ell ^*\in \mathrm{TC}(X,	heta ;\phi )$. We need to show that $\ell ^*\in \mathrm{TC}(X,	heta ;\phi )$. The equality $\ell ^*_{-\infty }=\phi $ was proved in 17.13.15 . Therefore, $\ell ^*\in \mathrm{TC}(X,	heta ;\phi )$ as expected. The two operations are inverse to each other thanks to corollary 21.11.26 . Hence